"The Lemmens–Seidel conjecture for base size 5 - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0195669826000855)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; In order to prove the Lemmens–Seidel conjecture, we need to show it for base sizes 3, 4, 5 and 6, where the base size will be given in Definition 2.1. ... Our proof of Theorem 1.2 uses the pillar method of Lemmens and Seidel.\nTheorem 1.1 The Lemmens–Seidel Conjecture\n\nFor $d\\ge 23$, ${N}_{1/5}\\left(d\\right)=max\\left\\{276,⌊(3d-3)/2⌋\\right\\}.$\n\nHere, a set of 276 equiangular lines with common angle $arccos(1/5)$ in dimension 23 is known to be unique [7, Theorem A]. In order to prove the Lemmens–Seidel conjecture, we need to show it for base sizes 3, 4, 5 and 6, where the base size will be given in Definition 2.1. In 1973, Lemmens and Seidel proved it for base size $6$ [15, Theorem 5.7]. In 2020, Lin and Yu proved the conjecture for base size 3 with a computer [16, Theorem 4.3], and claimed to prove it for base size $5$ [16, Theorem 4.6]. However, there is a gap in [16, Proof of Theorem 4.6 (1)]. This gap undermines their main approach, and therefore a different approach is needed. In 2022, Cao et al. proved the conjecture for base sizes 3 and 4 without a computer [4, Theorems 6.1, 7.5 and 9.3]. Hence, to complete a proof of the conjecture, we prove Theorem 1.2 by a different approach. This immediately implies the conjecture for base size 5.\n\nTheorem 1.2 The Lemmens–Seidel Conjecture for Base Size 5\n\nA set of $n$ equiangular lines with common angle $arccos(1/5)$ with base size 5 in dimension $d$ satisfies $n\\le max\\left\\{276,⌊(4d+36)/3⌋\\right\\}.$\n\nOur proof of Theorem 1.2 uses the pillar method of Lemmens and Seidel. The pillar method gives a partition of a set of equiangular lines into a base and pillars. A key point in the argument for base size 6 is that an upper bound on the cardinality of a pillar can be obtained if there exists another non-empty pillar. For base size 5, to obtain the required upper bound for a specific pillar, we need to consider two or three other pillars simultaneously. The choice of these pillars is crucial. In Corollary 5.2, we specify such a choice and prove the corresponding upper bound. We remark that, in this paper, the pillars are defined in terms of graphs rather than in terms of lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlexander N. Shumakovitch (https://cpb-us-e1.wpmucdn.com/blogs.gwu.edu/dist/2/298/files/2019/04/AShumakovitch-ResearchStatement_2018.pdf)\nciteturn7search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; homology groups, where dT is the Turner diﬀerential, β is the Bockstein diﬀerential, and ν ... Find an algebraic relation between Turner and Bockstein diﬀerentials on higher\nacyclic. In particular, ν∗establishes an isomorphism between the two non-trivial diagonals\nof the Khovanov homology over Z2 for Z2H-thin links. Because of this relation on d∗\nT and β,\nthe Bockstein spectral sequence collapses at the second page for Z2H-thin links as well.\nBut the story does not end here.\nd∗\nT and β are diﬀerentials on the ﬁrst pages in the\ncorresponding spectral sequence. One can ask whether there is a relation between diﬀerentials\non higher pages as well. Over the last couple of years, I have made several strides in this\ndirection, closing in on a formula that relates second-page diﬀerentials in these spectral\nsequences, but a general formulation is still elusive.\nProject 3. Find an algebraic relation between Turner and Bockstein diﬀerentials on higher\npages in the corresponding spectral sequences. Prove that if the Turner spectral sequence\ncollapses at the n-th page, then the Bockstein one does so as well.\nAs a corollary, one would conﬁrm that if a link has its mod-2 Khovanov homology sup-\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCanad. J. Math. 2021